Kyto's source is published so that it can be READ. People trust Kyto with their
messages, their credentials and their repositories, and "you can check what it
actually does" is a better answer to that trust than a promise. Being readable is
not the same as being free to take, and this licence is deliberately narrower than
an open-source one: you can study it and help improve it, but you cannot run or
host it — not commercially, not non-commercially.

1. WHAT YOU MAY DO

   You may view, read, and privately discuss this source code. You may quote
   short excerpts for the purpose of commentary, security review, teaching, or
   reporting a problem, with attribution.

2. CONTRIBUTIONS ARE WELCOME

   The restrictions in Section 3 are not meant to stop you from improving Kyto.
   Solely for the purpose of preparing and submitting a contribution to the
   canonical repository, you may:

   a. fork the repository on its hosting platform and create branches;
   b. modify the source; and
   c. build and run the software locally, in a private, non-production
      environment, only as far as needed to develop and test your change.

   These permissions end where contribution ends. They do not allow hosting or
   operating the software for yourself or others, keeping or using a modified
   version for any purpose other than the contribution, or distributing the
   software beyond the fork-and-pull-request flow.

   By submitting a contribution (for example, a pull request), you grant the
   copyright holder a perpetual, worldwide, irrevocable, royalty-free licence
   to use, modify, distribute, sublicense, and relicense that contribution as
   part of Kyto or otherwise. If you do not agree to that, do not submit.

3. WHAT YOU MAY NOT DO

   Except as Sections 1 and 2 allow, and without prior written permission from
   the copyright holder, you may not:

   a. copy, reproduce, or redistribute this software or any substantial portion
      of it, in source or compiled form;
   b. run, host, deploy, or operate this software, or any work derived from it,
      whether or not for a fee — including personal, internal, and
      non-commercial hosting;
   c. modify it, or create derivative works from it;
   d. incorporate any part of it into another work;
   e. sublicense, sell, rent, or otherwise transfer any rights in it;
   f. use it to train, fine-tune, evaluate, or otherwise develop a machine
      learning model.

   No licence is granted by implication, estoppel, or otherwise. Making the
   source publicly readable is not a grant of any of the rights withheld above.

4. GORKIE-DERIVED CODE IS STILL MIT

   Kyto began as a fork of gorkie, which was published under the MIT licence.
   Code in this repository that originated in gorkie — including this
   repository's history from before Kyto's rewrite — remains available under
   those MIT terms, which are reproduced in ./LICENSE-gorkie-MIT. This licence
   applies to Kyto's own code and cannot and does not withdraw permissions MIT
   already granted for that code. Section 3 is not a claim over gorkie's work.

5. THIRD-PARTY MATERIAL

   This repository vendors third-party material that carries its own licences,
   including the agent skills under ./.agents/skills/ and the dependencies
   declared in the package manifests. Nothing here alters those licences, and
   Section 3 does not apply to them.

